@DemisHassabis . @AgentGlassInc 
The prompt:
Build a single self-contained HTML file using Three.js loaded only from a pinned CDN version (an import map pointing "three" and "three/addons/" at https://t.co/58LurBjwx0) that renders an original East-Asian-inspired temple courtyard at golden hour, filling the whole window. Model everything procedurally in code, with no external models, textures or images: a five-tier pagoda with gently upturned eaves, dark roof tiles, red columns and a finial; an arched red wooden bridge over a still pond that reflects the sky and the pagoda; stone lanterns glowing warmly; a low courtyard wall with a gate; gravel paths; pine and maple trees with layered foliage and a few drifting leaves. Light it with a low warm sun casting long soft shadows, a gradient sky, ACES filmic tone mapping and a light atmospheric haze that softens the distance. A slow cinematic camera orbits the courtyard, completing a full circle in about 32 seconds at a gentle height, and the very first frame must already be a well-composed view of the whole scene (no loading screen, no fade-in). No text, labels or UI in the scene, and no real temple, place or country names. Keep it smooth at 1920x1080 and the code compact (under about 1,200 lines). It must run with zero console errors. Return only the complete HTML file. The camera path must stay outside every object: orbit at a radius and height that clear all trees, walls and buildings, always aimed at the pagoda.

As requested by many, here’s a concise guide on using Gemini 3.0 to reliably generate bold, unconventional and visually striking designs.

for this example i will be using the first one that i started with but with a twist. REFINEMENT (+4 Days)

